00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:34.400
It's important to note
that while what we're

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:37.070
talking about here
today is reasonably new,

00:01:37.070 --> 00:01:39.740
the mathematical foundations
upon which it's built

00:01:39.740 --> 00:01:41.720
have been around for millennia.

00:01:41.720 --> 00:01:45.650
The original graph
problem was solved

00:01:45.650 --> 00:01:48.950
by a Swiss mathematician
in the 1700s

00:01:48.950 --> 00:01:51.050
by the name of Leonhard Euler.

00:01:51.050 --> 00:01:53.510
And he solved the famous
intractable problem

00:01:53.510 --> 00:01:55.850
called the Bridges
of Koenigsberg

00:01:55.850 --> 00:02:00.120
by reducing it to what is now
known as modern graph theory.

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:03.890
It turns out that a lot of the
same things, same paradigms

00:02:03.890 --> 00:02:07.430
and concepts, that he
used to solve this problem

00:02:07.430 --> 00:02:12.470
are also useful for solving
your tricky Sudoku puzzle

00:02:12.470 --> 00:02:14.900
as well as determining
the fewest number of moves

00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:19.520
to swap positions
on a chess board.

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:26.060
A little more tangibly,
there are several real world

00:02:26.060 --> 00:02:29.700
algorithms that can be put to
use, clustering, betweenness,

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:31.040
similarity.

00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:33.560
We all know what page
rank is good for.

00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:37.760
I would recommend that you
look at some of the URLs

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:39.520
at the end of the presentation.

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:43.130
There are several great recipes
on the TinkerPop website

00:02:43.130 --> 00:02:45.680
with graph algorithms
and good use

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:47.660
cases as to when to use them.

00:02:50.810 --> 00:02:53.480
Graphs are modeled in the
real world all over the place.

00:02:53.480 --> 00:02:56.660
Transportation networks
can be modeled as graphs.

00:02:56.660 --> 00:03:00.020
Computer networks and IoT
can be modeled as graphs.

00:03:00.020 --> 00:03:03.170
The canonical social network
that we're all familiar with

00:03:03.170 --> 00:03:05.690
can be modeled in a graph.

00:03:05.690 --> 00:03:11.060
Interestingly, the first
commercial database, IBM's IMS,

00:03:11.060 --> 00:03:13.640
modeled a graph as
a bill of materials

00:03:13.640 --> 00:03:18.230
in the late '60s for
the Saturn V project.

00:03:18.230 --> 00:03:20.690
When we talk about
graph databases,

00:03:20.690 --> 00:03:23.960
one flavor is known as
the RDF triplestore.

00:03:23.960 --> 00:03:27.680
It stands for Resource
Description Format.

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:30.380
Coming out of research
around the semantic web

00:03:30.380 --> 00:03:34.880
in the late '90s, it consists of
a three-part tuple-- a subject,

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:36.380
predicate, and object.

00:03:36.380 --> 00:03:38.770
In this case, I've
got Brian is a person.

00:03:38.770 --> 00:03:40.390
Misha is a person.

00:03:40.390 --> 00:03:41.960
Brian knows Misha.

00:03:41.960 --> 00:03:45.170
These are all examples
of RDF triples.

00:03:45.170 --> 00:03:47.150
SPARQL is the query language.

00:03:47.150 --> 00:03:51.590
And this is not
what JanusGraph is.

00:03:51.590 --> 00:03:54.860
So the second category
of graph database

00:03:54.860 --> 00:03:57.440
is something called a
property graph, where you

00:03:57.440 --> 00:03:59.780
have both vertices and edges.

00:03:59.780 --> 00:04:03.440
Each of them may
have properties.

00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:05.430
And edges are directed.

00:04:05.430 --> 00:04:09.830
While SPARQL is the lingua
franca for RDF triples,

00:04:09.830 --> 00:04:12.770
there are various
query languages

00:04:12.770 --> 00:04:14.650
to be used against
property graphs.

00:04:14.650 --> 00:04:16.459
JanusGraph is a property graph.

00:04:16.459 --> 00:04:18.680
And the query language
that we use here

00:04:18.680 --> 00:04:21.740
is something called Gremlin.

00:04:21.740 --> 00:04:24.305
When we work with clients
all over the place,

00:04:24.305 --> 00:04:28.400
we try to pick the right
use case and ask them,

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:31.190
is the graph a good
fit for this use case?

00:04:31.190 --> 00:04:34.400
So usually it consists of a
couple different questions.

00:04:34.400 --> 00:04:37.160
Are there natural
networks in my data?

00:04:37.160 --> 00:04:40.130
And do I have to traverse
the graph in order

00:04:40.130 --> 00:04:42.860
to get the answers to
the questions I'm asking?

00:04:42.860 --> 00:04:44.690
Oftentimes, we
work with clients,

00:04:44.690 --> 00:04:47.210
and the answer to the
first question is yes,

00:04:47.210 --> 00:04:49.730
and the answer to the
second question is no.

00:04:49.730 --> 00:04:53.380
For instance, in the
supply chain use case,

00:04:53.380 --> 00:04:57.120
I might think that graph
would be a good application

00:04:57.120 --> 00:04:59.720
if I, for instance, was
looking to see an outage

00:04:59.720 --> 00:05:02.450
and how it might ripple
through my supply chain.

00:05:02.450 --> 00:05:05.030
But conversely, if I'm
trying to figure out

00:05:05.030 --> 00:05:07.790
what is the net sum
total of widgets

00:05:07.790 --> 00:05:09.950
in my suite of
warehouses, that's

00:05:09.950 --> 00:05:13.250
kind of a classic SQL use case.

00:05:13.250 --> 00:05:15.610
So it's always
important to make sure

00:05:15.610 --> 00:05:19.060
that you pick the right use
cases and the right problems

00:05:19.060 --> 00:05:22.390
when you deploy this technology.

00:05:22.390 --> 00:05:26.210
But when you do find
the right problem,

00:05:26.210 --> 00:05:30.120
it can be a very concise
and elegant solution

00:05:30.120 --> 00:05:31.870
to the questions you're asking.

00:05:31.870 --> 00:05:35.410
This example here is a dirt
simple recommendation engine,

00:05:35.410 --> 00:05:40.660
where on the left, you see a
14-table inner and outer join,

00:05:40.660 --> 00:05:44.260
and on the right, you see a
very concise Gremlin query.

00:05:44.260 --> 00:05:46.090
This is actually built
under the premise

00:05:46.090 --> 00:05:49.810
that if you and I have nine of
the same products in common,

00:05:49.810 --> 00:05:52.930
and I have the 10th one,
you're likely to also want

00:05:52.930 --> 00:05:53.947
that 10th one.

00:05:56.570 --> 00:05:59.930
What we also find in many
cases is marrying a graph

00:05:59.930 --> 00:06:02.840
database with a
time series data can

00:06:02.840 --> 00:06:05.750
be a very powerful paradigm.

00:06:05.750 --> 00:06:07.820
What we end up
modeling frequently

00:06:07.820 --> 00:06:10.370
are atomic unit
snapshots in time,

00:06:10.370 --> 00:06:13.670
where I'm capturing a
stock price on a given day

00:06:13.670 --> 00:06:15.830
or a reading on an IoT sensor.

00:06:15.830 --> 00:06:17.275
And then as I stack
them together,

00:06:17.275 --> 00:06:18.690
then I've got a time series.

00:06:18.690 --> 00:06:21.530
So this gives me the
micro view of what

00:06:21.530 --> 00:06:23.700
is happening at any
given point in time

00:06:23.700 --> 00:06:26.120
and how it trends over time.

00:06:26.120 --> 00:06:28.820
When I marry that with a
graph, what I can also do

00:06:28.820 --> 00:06:32.390
is get a bigger sense of how
these things are interrelated

00:06:32.390 --> 00:06:35.120
and possibly
understand causality.

00:06:35.120 --> 00:06:37.760
Why is this thing trending
up while the other thing

00:06:37.760 --> 00:06:39.110
is trending down?

00:06:39.110 --> 00:06:43.970
If I see either of them on their
own, I don't have that context.

00:06:43.970 --> 00:06:46.190
If I see it modeled as
a graph in conjunction

00:06:46.190 --> 00:06:49.040
with the time
series, then I might

00:06:49.040 --> 00:06:50.600
be able to understand
the relations

00:06:50.600 --> 00:06:54.780
and possibly even predict where
things might go in the future.

00:06:54.780 --> 00:07:00.720
So how does this manifest itself
in use cases in the real world?

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:05.330
So for these use cases that
we're going to walk through,

00:07:05.330 --> 00:07:09.140
one in each of the industries,
we'll outline the problem.

00:07:09.140 --> 00:07:12.170
We'll walk through a little bit
about how you would model it

00:07:12.170 --> 00:07:14.060
in a property graph.

00:07:14.060 --> 00:07:17.630
We'll walk through the use case
of how we exploit this data

00:07:17.630 --> 00:07:21.050
model, so that you understand
the processing involved.

00:07:21.050 --> 00:07:22.530
And then in a
couple of examples,

00:07:22.530 --> 00:07:24.155
we'll walk through
some user interfaces

00:07:24.155 --> 00:07:25.800
about how this would
manifest itself

00:07:25.800 --> 00:07:28.762
if you were going to
build a finished product.

00:07:28.762 --> 00:07:30.470
As a reminder, all
the things that you're

00:07:30.470 --> 00:07:33.170
going to see here
in a second, these

00:07:33.170 --> 00:07:38.290
are all property graph models
with vertices and edges.

00:07:38.290 --> 00:07:41.750
And each of them
may have properties.

00:07:41.750 --> 00:07:43.929
Edges are directed.

00:07:43.929 --> 00:07:45.720
Some of these graphs
are a little bit busy.

00:07:45.720 --> 00:07:49.830
So we've dropped some of the
properties just for clarity.

00:07:49.830 --> 00:07:52.010
So how would I do this
in an IoT setting?

00:07:54.850 --> 00:07:56.730
This model that I'm
going to walk through

00:07:56.730 --> 00:07:59.690
is useful and can be
kind of translated

00:07:59.690 --> 00:08:02.610
in several different scenarios.

00:08:02.610 --> 00:08:04.610
One would be computer networks.

00:08:04.610 --> 00:08:06.980
I could model this as
sensors in the oil and gas

00:08:06.980 --> 00:08:10.550
industry, industrial
networks, power grids,

00:08:10.550 --> 00:08:12.450
those sorts of things.

00:08:12.450 --> 00:08:14.090
So questions we might
be able to answer

00:08:14.090 --> 00:08:19.400
in this setting would be, what
does this service actually

00:08:19.400 --> 00:08:20.120
depend on?

00:08:20.120 --> 00:08:24.990
You saw the Istio use cases
earlier today at the keynote.

00:08:24.990 --> 00:08:27.935
There's some similarities
to what you'll see here.

00:08:27.935 --> 00:08:29.390
If I'm trying to
find the shortest

00:08:29.390 --> 00:08:33.544
path between two nodes in a
network, how would I find that?

00:08:33.544 --> 00:08:35.419
And then the one that
we'll walk through here

00:08:35.419 --> 00:08:39.080
is, what is the impact of
a service outage in my SaaS

00:08:39.080 --> 00:08:41.270
application?

00:08:41.270 --> 00:08:44.000
So imagine I'm modeling
an infrastructure.

00:08:44.000 --> 00:08:45.830
I've got a SaaS application.

00:08:45.830 --> 00:08:47.540
And this SaaS
application consists

00:08:47.540 --> 00:08:51.140
of a couple of web interfaces
and maybe an external API

00:08:51.140 --> 00:08:53.480
that my clients are consuming.

00:08:53.480 --> 00:08:58.490
Each of these in turn is
reliant on internal APIs.

00:08:58.490 --> 00:09:03.057
And those APIs are reliant
on other internal APIs.

00:09:03.057 --> 00:09:04.640
At the end of the
day, eventually I've

00:09:04.640 --> 00:09:06.650
got to get data from somewhere.

00:09:06.650 --> 00:09:09.250
And a couple of these APIs
are querying a database,

00:09:09.250 --> 00:09:13.190
and one of them is
querying a search index.

00:09:13.190 --> 00:09:16.340
So when the system's
healthy, everybody's green.

00:09:16.340 --> 00:09:19.520
But imagine I've got
some spike in the CPU

00:09:19.520 --> 00:09:20.900
on one of my databases, right?

00:09:20.900 --> 00:09:24.440
I see a time series getting
woven into my graph model,

00:09:24.440 --> 00:09:28.850
and I see the CPU has spiked.

00:09:28.850 --> 00:09:31.460
Clearly, if that
database spikes,

00:09:31.460 --> 00:09:34.640
then this API is
going to be affected,

00:09:34.640 --> 00:09:37.521
the other upstream
API will be affected,

00:09:37.521 --> 00:09:39.020
and then my web
interfaces are going

00:09:39.020 --> 00:09:41.550
to go out or be in trouble.

00:09:41.550 --> 00:09:45.410
So while this might be a
very obvious easy to eyeball

00:09:45.410 --> 00:09:49.370
scenario in a 12-node
network, imagine this

00:09:49.370 --> 00:09:52.520
if you've got hundreds or
even thousands of services.

00:09:52.520 --> 00:09:54.440
And how would I
predict what's going

00:09:54.440 --> 00:09:57.610
to happen based on some outage?

00:09:57.610 --> 00:09:59.840
I might use this
information in order

00:09:59.840 --> 00:10:02.150
to deploy limited
extra resources.

00:10:02.150 --> 00:10:03.320
Maybe I've got budget.

00:10:03.320 --> 00:10:04.820
Where's my biggest
point of failure?

00:10:04.820 --> 00:10:06.320
Where's my susceptibility?

00:10:06.320 --> 00:10:11.450
Or where's my anchor client
going to get affected?

00:10:11.450 --> 00:10:14.190
I might want to put
whatever extra budget

00:10:14.190 --> 00:10:19.170
I have toward making sure
that that one is bulletproof.

00:10:19.170 --> 00:10:23.610
Here's a mockup
generated by us that

00:10:23.610 --> 00:10:25.130
walks through this use case.

00:10:25.130 --> 00:10:28.250
You see an Elasticsearch,
a node that's laboring,

00:10:28.250 --> 00:10:32.510
and it's affecting JanusGraph
and a Spring Boot API going

00:10:32.510 --> 00:10:33.120
upstream.

00:10:33.120 --> 00:10:36.560
You see some tabular data on the
right telling us a little bit

00:10:36.560 --> 00:10:40.970
about what alarm has
gone off and why.

00:10:40.970 --> 00:10:43.460
And down at the bottom
here on the next,

00:10:43.460 --> 00:10:45.710
you see some sort
of a time frame

00:10:45.710 --> 00:10:49.520
that I might be zooming
forward or back.

00:10:49.520 --> 00:10:51.410
Maybe I could rewind
the clock a little bit

00:10:51.410 --> 00:10:55.820
and figure out what was it that
happened last hour that caused

00:10:55.820 --> 00:11:00.550
my Elasticsearch node to spike.

00:11:00.550 --> 00:11:03.660
How might I do this
in supply chain?

00:11:03.660 --> 00:11:07.980
So in supply chain, there
are several graphs that

00:11:07.980 --> 00:11:10.320
can be modeled in this space.

00:11:10.320 --> 00:11:12.750
You've got the
suppliers and how they

00:11:12.750 --> 00:11:14.620
interoperate with each other.

00:11:14.620 --> 00:11:18.210
You've got the actual bill of
materials of the finished good.

00:11:18.210 --> 00:11:20.460
It consists of
some raw materials,

00:11:20.460 --> 00:11:23.940
and some subcomponents, and
subcomponents of subcomponents.

00:11:23.940 --> 00:11:25.410
You've got
transportation networks.

00:11:25.410 --> 00:11:30.970
How does this actually
get from factory to shelf?

00:11:30.970 --> 00:11:33.167
So questions that
we like to ask here

00:11:33.167 --> 00:11:35.250
are things like, what would
happen if, God forbid,

00:11:35.250 --> 00:11:36.120
there was a--

00:11:36.120 --> 00:11:39.630
to shipments-- if there's
an earthquake here today?

00:11:39.630 --> 00:11:42.090
Where are my bottlenecks
in my supplier network?

00:11:42.090 --> 00:11:44.720
Again, trying to
address extra budget.

00:11:44.720 --> 00:11:48.360
Maybe I can do something to help
bulletproof my supply chain.

00:11:48.360 --> 00:11:51.700
And then for those
of us from Texas,

00:11:51.700 --> 00:11:54.060
what finished products
are affected when

00:11:54.060 --> 00:11:57.330
we have a flood in Houston?

00:11:57.330 --> 00:12:00.070
So here's a contrived
supplier network.

00:12:00.070 --> 00:12:03.000
Imagine I've got some
cars that I'm assembling.

00:12:03.000 --> 00:12:05.280
And I've got engines
and cylinders.

00:12:05.280 --> 00:12:10.170
I've got blocks, assemblers,
and finished products.

00:12:10.170 --> 00:12:11.650
I've also got a
bill of material.

00:12:11.650 --> 00:12:14.250
In this case, I've
got an engine that's

00:12:14.250 --> 00:12:18.090
composed of a block and a
head inside of the block.

00:12:18.090 --> 00:12:20.850
I've got pistons and cylinders.

00:12:20.850 --> 00:12:24.120
If I put the two
together, then what I see

00:12:24.120 --> 00:12:30.060
is the bill of material that may
flow through my supply chain.

00:12:30.060 --> 00:12:35.430
So here's your
time series again.

00:12:35.430 --> 00:12:37.380
It turns out each
of these suppliers,

00:12:37.380 --> 00:12:39.780
they have inventory on hand.

00:12:39.780 --> 00:12:45.570
So I've got some of my
engines on the shelf already.

00:12:45.570 --> 00:12:49.620
And then I want to
simulate this disruption.

00:12:49.620 --> 00:12:54.000
So imagine this supplier down
at the bottom is in Houston.

00:12:54.000 --> 00:12:56.580
Obviously, he goes out.

00:12:56.580 --> 00:12:58.800
The supply lane
actually gets affected,

00:12:58.800 --> 00:13:01.080
as does that next supplier.

00:13:01.080 --> 00:13:03.780
This supplier here is
affected, because as you see,

00:13:03.780 --> 00:13:06.510
he's got no inventory on
hand in September, right?

00:13:06.510 --> 00:13:10.860
He cannot weather the
storm metaphorically.

00:13:10.860 --> 00:13:14.460
This other supplier, he actually
has some inventory on hand.

00:13:14.460 --> 00:13:20.130
So he's got some capacity
to continue to meet his SLAs

00:13:20.130 --> 00:13:21.930
even after the flood.

00:13:21.930 --> 00:13:23.790
You also see the
bottom supplier.

00:13:23.790 --> 00:13:25.530
He's feeding into this supplier.

00:13:25.530 --> 00:13:26.920
So he's unaffected as well.

00:13:26.920 --> 00:13:30.930
So here you see, again, where
the time series in the graph

00:13:30.930 --> 00:13:35.400
tells you a better
view of the whole.

00:13:35.400 --> 00:13:38.570
Here's another visual mockup
of supply chain disruption

00:13:38.570 --> 00:13:39.930
simulator.

00:13:39.930 --> 00:13:42.270
This is on our website,
where you can actually

00:13:42.270 --> 00:13:47.790
geocode, and draw simulated
outages based on your supplier

00:13:47.790 --> 00:13:53.100
network, and simulate
the effects of an outage

00:13:53.100 --> 00:13:57.990
and how you might want to try to
reroute some of your supplies.

00:13:57.990 --> 00:14:01.950
How might we do this in a
financial services setting?

00:14:01.950 --> 00:14:05.430
In financial services,
you see graph identifying

00:14:05.430 --> 00:14:07.020
fraudulent activity.

00:14:07.020 --> 00:14:10.530
You can use the
social network model

00:14:10.530 --> 00:14:12.960
for criminal network detection.

00:14:12.960 --> 00:14:16.650
And then in this one, we'll
walk through evaluating

00:14:16.650 --> 00:14:19.150
complex structured products.

00:14:19.150 --> 00:14:24.210
So imagine, how would
I consider valuing

00:14:24.210 --> 00:14:26.280
something that's not
overly obvious what

00:14:26.280 --> 00:14:28.050
the current value is?

00:14:28.050 --> 00:14:29.670
So in the first two
examples, we were

00:14:29.670 --> 00:14:33.641
talking through use cases that
might be consumed by a finished

00:14:33.641 --> 00:14:34.140
product.

00:14:34.140 --> 00:14:37.170
But in this case, imagine you've
got a data scientist who's

00:14:37.170 --> 00:14:39.390
testing some theses.

00:14:39.390 --> 00:14:42.350
He's got lots of data, some
public, some not so public.

00:14:42.350 --> 00:14:45.720
And he's trying
to figure out, how

00:14:45.720 --> 00:14:48.400
do I value something based on
all of these different data

00:14:48.400 --> 00:14:52.780
sets that I might not be
able to assimilate just yet?

00:14:52.780 --> 00:14:55.380
So in this case, we're
going to value a REIT.

00:14:55.380 --> 00:14:57.510
For those of you who
don't know what a REIT is,

00:14:57.510 --> 00:15:01.170
it's essentially a Real
Estate Investment Trust.

00:15:01.170 --> 00:15:03.180
And what that means
is it's a big basket

00:15:03.180 --> 00:15:08.700
of real estate properties
that you value in totality.

00:15:08.700 --> 00:15:11.410
So in our REIT, we've
got two properties.

00:15:11.410 --> 00:15:13.570
One property is a
shopping mall in Oklahoma,

00:15:13.570 --> 00:15:17.460
and the other one is a
water park in Florida.

00:15:17.460 --> 00:15:21.990
And we've been able to
model that the shopping

00:15:21.990 --> 00:15:24.300
mall in Oklahoma
actually is managed

00:15:24.300 --> 00:15:26.520
by a professional
property manager

00:15:26.520 --> 00:15:30.720
and also has two
tenants that are in it.

00:15:30.720 --> 00:15:33.810
We've got some public data
about the value of this REIT.

00:15:33.810 --> 00:15:36.990
And we see that
it is trending up.

00:15:36.990 --> 00:15:40.650
And possibly what I've also
got is some not so public data,

00:15:40.650 --> 00:15:45.150
where I've got a way to
capture foot traffic.

00:15:45.150 --> 00:15:50.201
Maybe this node at the
bottom is my anchor tenant.

00:15:50.201 --> 00:15:52.200
And I've now been able
to determine that there's

00:15:52.200 --> 00:15:56.110
some correlation between foot
traffic and my anchor tenant

00:15:56.110 --> 00:15:59.010
and the value of my
publicly-traded REIT.

00:15:59.010 --> 00:16:01.500
Maybe if I've tested
enough theses,

00:16:01.500 --> 00:16:05.760
I'm confident enough
in that hypothesis

00:16:05.760 --> 00:16:10.180
that I might be willing to
trade ahead of that information.

00:16:10.180 --> 00:16:15.750
So hopefully you see a
couple of different use cases

00:16:15.750 --> 00:16:21.570
and scenarios where coupling the
graph with the time series data

00:16:21.570 --> 00:16:23.550
is very powerful.

00:16:23.550 --> 00:16:27.250
And now I'm going to
turn it over to Misha.

00:16:27.250 --> 00:16:30.220
He's going to tell
you how this actually

00:16:30.220 --> 00:16:32.847
can come to life on GCP.

00:16:32.847 --> 00:16:34.180
MISHA BRUKMAN: Thank you, Brian.

00:16:37.760 --> 00:16:39.750
So as we mentioned
earlier, JanusGraph

00:16:39.750 --> 00:16:43.050
is an open source scalable
distributed graph database.

00:16:43.050 --> 00:16:44.550
It's an Apache licensed project.

00:16:47.450 --> 00:16:49.740
It's backed by the
Linux Foundation.

00:16:49.740 --> 00:16:53.370
There are a number of users and
developers of this project now.

00:16:53.370 --> 00:16:54.530
It's quite popular.

00:16:54.530 --> 00:16:57.410
And it's growing very quickly.

00:16:57.410 --> 00:17:01.650
JanusGraph provides a variety
of support for different storage

00:17:01.650 --> 00:17:03.500
back ends as well as
indexing back ends.

00:17:03.500 --> 00:17:06.470
And here we're going to
explore how to use JanusGraph

00:17:06.470 --> 00:17:09.079
with a Bigtable
back end for storage

00:17:09.079 --> 00:17:13.050
and Elasticsearch for indexing.

00:17:13.050 --> 00:17:14.310
Brian alluded to this earlier.

00:17:14.310 --> 00:17:16.940
So there's a project
called Apache TinkerPop.

00:17:16.940 --> 00:17:18.890
And TinkerPop itself
is also an Apache

00:17:18.890 --> 00:17:21.170
licensed open source project.

00:17:21.170 --> 00:17:24.470
And it provides the framework
for open source graph

00:17:24.470 --> 00:17:26.210
modeling and computation.

00:17:26.210 --> 00:17:28.880
And it is hosted at the
Apache Software Foundation.

00:17:28.880 --> 00:17:30.860
It also provides the
Gremlin query language

00:17:30.860 --> 00:17:33.700
that is used for querying
these property graphs.

00:17:33.700 --> 00:17:36.120
As Brian mentioned, JanusGraph
supports the property graph

00:17:36.120 --> 00:17:36.620
model.

00:17:36.620 --> 00:17:39.400
And all the examples that you
saw are property graph models.

00:17:39.400 --> 00:17:42.390
Gremlin is quite a popular
querying language for graphs.

00:17:42.390 --> 00:17:47.557
And it's supported by over
15 popular graph databases.

00:17:47.557 --> 00:17:49.390
Here's an example of
deployment architecture

00:17:49.390 --> 00:17:53.505
of how you can deploy a
scalable graph database on GCP.

00:17:53.505 --> 00:17:54.880
So there's a
solution white paper

00:17:54.880 --> 00:17:56.320
that we've published at the URL.

00:17:56.320 --> 00:17:58.790
And what you'll see is
that in that solution,

00:17:58.790 --> 00:18:01.810
JanusGraph is running
containerized on GKE

00:18:01.810 --> 00:18:03.460
together with Elasticsearch.

00:18:03.460 --> 00:18:06.340
And JanusGraph connects
to Elastic for indexing.

00:18:06.340 --> 00:18:08.887
And it's using Cloud Bigtable
as the storage back end.

00:18:08.887 --> 00:18:10.720
And in front of it,
there's a load balancer.

00:18:10.720 --> 00:18:13.750
So you would run a number of
different JanusGraph instances,

00:18:13.750 --> 00:18:16.964
which themselves are stateless,
and your applications would

00:18:16.964 --> 00:18:19.630
talk to the load balancer and be
routed to one of the JanusGraph

00:18:19.630 --> 00:18:21.760
instances.

00:18:21.760 --> 00:18:23.060
So what is Cloud Bigtable?

00:18:23.060 --> 00:18:25.610
"Bigtable is a sparse,
distributed, persistent,

00:18:25.610 --> 00:18:27.069
multidimensional sorted map."

00:18:27.069 --> 00:18:28.610
That's the description
from the paper

00:18:28.610 --> 00:18:30.290
that we published in 2006.

00:18:30.290 --> 00:18:33.800
Let's dive into some of the
details behind what this is.

00:18:33.800 --> 00:18:36.600
So Bigtable is a distributed
key multi-value store.

00:18:36.600 --> 00:18:40.140
So for a single key, you can
store many different values.

00:18:40.140 --> 00:18:42.470
Bigtable is sparse in that
if you don't write data

00:18:42.470 --> 00:18:44.180
in particular in
a number of cells,

00:18:44.180 --> 00:18:46.320
it does not cost you
any storage space.

00:18:46.320 --> 00:18:48.920
So you can store data in rows
that is relevant to them,

00:18:48.920 --> 00:18:51.820
and you don't need to fill out
every single cell with null

00:18:51.820 --> 00:18:53.780
or placeholder values.

00:18:53.780 --> 00:18:55.700
Bigtable supports
atomic operations.

00:18:55.700 --> 00:18:59.390
So you can modify any single
set of values in a single row,

00:18:59.390 --> 00:19:01.820
in a single transaction,
and that will be atomic.

00:19:01.820 --> 00:19:05.000
Bigtable provides a single
index, which is the row key,

00:19:05.000 --> 00:19:07.050
and the row keys are
stored in sorted order.

00:19:07.050 --> 00:19:09.110
So you have a sorted map.

00:19:09.110 --> 00:19:12.890
Here you see an illustration
of what a subset of a table

00:19:12.890 --> 00:19:14.180
would be in Bigtable.

00:19:14.180 --> 00:19:17.060
And the various values here
are how you would address them.

00:19:17.060 --> 00:19:18.500
So there are column families.

00:19:18.500 --> 00:19:20.270
And inside them are
column qualifiers.

00:19:20.270 --> 00:19:22.280
And the way to
address a single cell

00:19:22.280 --> 00:19:25.350
is with the combination of
row, column family, and column

00:19:25.350 --> 00:19:27.170
qualifier.

00:19:27.170 --> 00:19:30.680
And that said, the map
actually also includes

00:19:30.680 --> 00:19:31.950
a timestamp dimension.

00:19:31.950 --> 00:19:35.300
So the full mapping
of values in Bigtable

00:19:35.300 --> 00:19:37.310
is with a row key,
a column, which

00:19:37.310 --> 00:19:38.780
is a combination
of column family

00:19:38.780 --> 00:19:41.810
and an optional qualifier,
and a 64-bit timestamp.

00:19:41.810 --> 00:19:45.320
And the values in Bigtable
are uninterpreted byte arrays.

00:19:45.320 --> 00:19:48.770
So you can store anything
from JSON to protocol buffers

00:19:48.770 --> 00:19:50.690
to snippets of
audio or thumbnails

00:19:50.690 --> 00:19:55.490
or anything that is relevant
to your particular use case.

00:19:55.490 --> 00:19:57.540
Every cell in Bigtable,
as I mentioned,

00:19:57.540 --> 00:19:59.550
has an int64 attached to it.

00:19:59.550 --> 00:20:02.030
So the way to think about is
is that Bigtable's actually

00:20:02.030 --> 00:20:03.650
a three-dimensional database.

00:20:03.650 --> 00:20:06.080
Every cell can store a
number of different versions,

00:20:06.080 --> 00:20:08.954
and they do not have to match
across the different cells

00:20:08.954 --> 00:20:10.370
in the same row
or different rows.

00:20:10.370 --> 00:20:12.710
So you can store
different versions

00:20:12.710 --> 00:20:14.480
of the data, which
means you can store

00:20:14.480 --> 00:20:16.280
time series within each cell.

00:20:16.280 --> 00:20:19.460
So Bigtable comes by default
with first-class support

00:20:19.460 --> 00:20:20.930
for time series.

00:20:20.930 --> 00:20:23.690
And you can also have an
optional garbage collection

00:20:23.690 --> 00:20:24.450
enabled for this.

00:20:24.450 --> 00:20:27.197
So you can keep writing
data, and older data

00:20:27.197 --> 00:20:29.780
will automatically expire and
be garbage collected and removed

00:20:29.780 --> 00:20:30.280
for you.

00:20:33.010 --> 00:20:35.080
Let's talk a bit about
how the requests are

00:20:35.080 --> 00:20:35.960
routed in Bigtable.

00:20:35.960 --> 00:20:38.230
So clients connect to
a single end point,

00:20:38.230 --> 00:20:40.704
which is a combination of
load balancing and proxy

00:20:40.704 --> 00:20:42.370
layer to Bigtable,
before they're routed

00:20:42.370 --> 00:20:44.336
to Bigtable server nodes.

00:20:44.336 --> 00:20:45.710
In the following
couple slides, I

00:20:45.710 --> 00:20:47.668
will remove the load
balancing and proxy layer.

00:20:47.668 --> 00:20:49.347
But keep in mind that
it is still there,

00:20:49.347 --> 00:20:51.180
and clients do not
actually directly connect

00:20:51.180 --> 00:20:53.200
to Bigtable nodes, which
means they don't need

00:20:53.200 --> 00:20:55.060
to do any discovery,
they don't need

00:20:55.060 --> 00:20:58.430
to update anything based on the
membership of a given cluster.

00:21:00.962 --> 00:21:02.670
Clients connect to
Bigtable server nodes.

00:21:02.670 --> 00:21:04.480
But Bigtable itself
is actually stateless.

00:21:04.480 --> 00:21:07.380
The data in Bigtable is actually
stored in our distributed file

00:21:07.380 --> 00:21:10.050
system, which is Colossus, which
is the successor to the Google

00:21:10.050 --> 00:21:11.430
File System.

00:21:11.430 --> 00:21:14.235
And here you can see the
sections of data, A, B, C,

00:21:14.235 --> 00:21:17.981
D, and E, which each of those
is a subset of a single Bigtable

00:21:17.981 --> 00:21:18.480
table.

00:21:18.480 --> 00:21:21.930
So each one of those
stores a sorted subset

00:21:21.930 --> 00:21:23.760
of the keys in that table.

00:21:23.760 --> 00:21:26.490
Also note that each
Bigtable node owns

00:21:26.490 --> 00:21:30.290
a number of these data regions,
and it has exclusive access

00:21:30.290 --> 00:21:30.790
to them.

00:21:30.790 --> 00:21:34.140
So this is what allows Bigtable
to provide atomic updates

00:21:34.140 --> 00:21:35.110
within a single row.

00:21:35.110 --> 00:21:37.890
So an entire single row is
contained in exactly one

00:21:37.890 --> 00:21:39.120
of those locations.

00:21:39.120 --> 00:21:41.070
And exactly one
Bigtable server node

00:21:41.070 --> 00:21:43.440
is responsible for
managing that data,

00:21:43.440 --> 00:21:46.020
both from a read and
write perspective.

00:21:46.020 --> 00:21:48.930
So now imagine we have one of
the server nodes is getting

00:21:48.930 --> 00:21:51.270
quite a lot of activity
from the clients,

00:21:51.270 --> 00:21:53.830
because it is managing a number
of the popular data sets.

00:21:53.830 --> 00:21:56.850
So it's Managing A, B, and C,
and so it gets more traffic

00:21:56.850 --> 00:22:00.360
from the clients
than other nodes.

00:22:00.360 --> 00:22:02.070
What we can easily
do is just reassign

00:22:02.070 --> 00:22:04.440
the ownership of the data
from one node to another.

00:22:04.440 --> 00:22:06.389
Note that there's no
data motion taking place

00:22:06.389 --> 00:22:08.430
because none of the nodes
themselves are actually

00:22:08.430 --> 00:22:09.900
storing the data locally.

00:22:09.900 --> 00:22:11.560
They provide
support for caching,

00:22:11.560 --> 00:22:15.270
but there's no durable storage
at the Bigtable node server

00:22:15.270 --> 00:22:16.030
level.

00:22:16.030 --> 00:22:17.211
The data stays in Colossus.

00:22:17.211 --> 00:22:18.960
There's no movement
regardless of how many

00:22:18.960 --> 00:22:20.820
gigabytes or terabytes
you're storing.

00:22:20.820 --> 00:22:22.320
And this reassignment
is very quick.

00:22:22.320 --> 00:22:23.520
And the clients
don't even notice.

00:22:23.520 --> 00:22:24.930
And again, they
don't need to know

00:22:24.930 --> 00:22:27.138
that they need to connect
to a different server node,

00:22:27.138 --> 00:22:30.660
because their requests
are transparently routed

00:22:30.660 --> 00:22:33.180
to a different server
in response to Bigtable

00:22:33.180 --> 00:22:35.340
behind the scenes
doing the optimization

00:22:35.340 --> 00:22:38.730
to rebalance the data such that
we provide good performance

00:22:38.730 --> 00:22:40.320
regardless of the scale.

00:22:40.320 --> 00:22:45.300
Now, given that we're able
to easily reassign the data

00:22:45.300 --> 00:22:47.260
ownership from one
node to another,

00:22:47.260 --> 00:22:49.020
this means that it's
quite easy for us

00:22:49.020 --> 00:22:52.350
to scale the cluster up and
down by adding or removing nodes

00:22:52.350 --> 00:22:54.150
to the cluster and
from the cluster.

00:22:54.150 --> 00:22:56.550
Because, again, there's
no data stored durably

00:22:56.550 --> 00:22:58.877
on a Bigtable node, this
means they are stateless.

00:22:58.877 --> 00:23:00.960
And if they're stateless,
we can easily move them.

00:23:00.960 --> 00:23:02.640
So the only thing
that they store

00:23:02.640 --> 00:23:05.370
is a cache of knowing what
it is that they're currently

00:23:05.370 --> 00:23:08.040
responsible for, which
we can easily reassign.

00:23:08.040 --> 00:23:12.685
So this means also that a
Bigtable node disappearing

00:23:12.685 --> 00:23:14.060
means that there
is no data loss.

00:23:14.060 --> 00:23:17.190
So Bigtable has no single
point of failure here.

00:23:17.190 --> 00:23:19.710
If you lose a node,
there is no data loss,

00:23:19.710 --> 00:23:22.895
because the data is replicated
and stored durably in Colossus.

00:23:25.740 --> 00:23:28.740
Because the nodes don't talk to
each other in Cloud Bigtable,

00:23:28.740 --> 00:23:31.840
Bigtable provides linear
scalability and performance.

00:23:31.840 --> 00:23:33.690
As you add nodes to
a Bigtable cluster

00:23:33.690 --> 00:23:36.630
to make the cluster larger,
you get linear scalability

00:23:36.630 --> 00:23:39.360
and throughput in
performance, because the nodes

00:23:39.360 --> 00:23:42.480
don't do any coordination,
because the transactions only

00:23:42.480 --> 00:23:45.930
happen locally at a single
Bigtable server node, which

00:23:45.930 --> 00:23:49.611
means also we can scale up
and down without any downtime,

00:23:49.611 --> 00:23:51.360
again, because of the
dynamic reassignment

00:23:51.360 --> 00:23:56.450
of the data from one node to
another behind the scenes.

00:23:56.450 --> 00:23:58.290
Today we're really
proud to announce

00:23:58.290 --> 00:24:01.660
that we're launching replication
to general availability.

00:24:01.660 --> 00:24:05.140
So this means that within
a Bigtable instance,

00:24:05.140 --> 00:24:08.020
you can create more than
one Bigtable cluster.

00:24:08.020 --> 00:24:10.890
And when you write data
once to a single location,

00:24:10.890 --> 00:24:13.260
it will be replicated
asynchronously

00:24:13.260 --> 00:24:14.840
to a different cluster.

00:24:14.840 --> 00:24:17.230
A single cluster provides
strong consistency in reads

00:24:17.230 --> 00:24:18.120
and writes.

00:24:18.120 --> 00:24:20.310
Once you switch to a
multi-cluster model,

00:24:20.310 --> 00:24:22.770
it will be eventually
consistent across clusters.

00:24:22.770 --> 00:24:24.330
There are no manual
steps that you

00:24:24.330 --> 00:24:26.719
need to take to ensure
data is replicated.

00:24:26.719 --> 00:24:29.010
It will be done for you
automatically and synchronized.

00:24:29.010 --> 00:24:30.180
There are no repair steps.

00:24:30.180 --> 00:24:31.181
There are no management.

00:24:31.181 --> 00:24:32.596
There's literally
nothing you need

00:24:32.596 --> 00:24:34.770
to do other than to say,
please add another cluster

00:24:34.770 --> 00:24:35.940
to this instance.

00:24:35.940 --> 00:24:38.970
And the use cases that this
is quite applicable for

00:24:38.970 --> 00:24:40.680
are both
high-availability serving

00:24:40.680 --> 00:24:43.410
for low-latency use
cases, as well as

00:24:43.410 --> 00:24:46.160
for separating analytics
from server use cases,

00:24:46.160 --> 00:24:48.600
such that you don't impact
your serving traffic

00:24:48.600 --> 00:24:51.000
from large-scale analytics.

00:24:51.000 --> 00:24:54.630
And this is going to be GA
as of tomorrow, July 25.

00:24:54.630 --> 00:24:59.160
It will increase the SLA
from 99.5% to 99.95%.

00:24:59.160 --> 00:25:01.970
And there's zero-touch failover
for high-availability use

00:25:01.970 --> 00:25:02.470
cases.

00:25:05.140 --> 00:25:07.330
As I mentioned, the
Bigtable data model

00:25:07.330 --> 00:25:09.130
supports keys in sorted order.

00:25:09.130 --> 00:25:11.549
And then there's a bunch
of cells that in each row,

00:25:11.549 --> 00:25:12.340
you can store data.

00:25:12.340 --> 00:25:14.500
And so you have cells,
and there's columns,

00:25:14.500 --> 00:25:18.490
and the data there
is sorted by column.

00:25:18.490 --> 00:25:21.870
And so JanusGraph, the way
it stores a graph in Bigtable

00:25:21.870 --> 00:25:25.660
is by storing the vertices
as the row keys in Bigtable.

00:25:25.660 --> 00:25:27.490
And as Brian
mentioned, there are

00:25:27.490 --> 00:25:30.830
properties on both the
vertices as well as the edges.

00:25:30.830 --> 00:25:33.070
And so the properties on
the vertices as well as

00:25:33.070 --> 00:25:35.200
the edges and the
properties of the edges

00:25:35.200 --> 00:25:38.284
are all stored within the
row for a single vertex.

00:25:38.284 --> 00:25:39.700
This makes it
really easy and fast

00:25:39.700 --> 00:25:41.800
to find an arbitrary
vertex and then find

00:25:41.800 --> 00:25:43.994
the edges that are
outgoing from that vertex,

00:25:43.994 --> 00:25:45.910
because they're in the
row, and they're always

00:25:45.910 --> 00:25:47.743
stored in that row
together with the vertex.

00:25:50.260 --> 00:25:52.480
Brian also mentioned the
applicability of time series

00:25:52.480 --> 00:25:55.060
data to a number of
these use cases from IoT

00:25:55.060 --> 00:25:56.980
to finance to supply chain.

00:25:56.980 --> 00:26:00.460
OpenTSDB is a very popular
open source time series

00:26:00.460 --> 00:26:04.150
database that stores millisecond
level precision time series

00:26:04.150 --> 00:26:05.050
data.

00:26:05.050 --> 00:26:07.030
And it scales
horizontally to support

00:26:07.030 --> 00:26:09.280
millions of writes per second.

00:26:09.280 --> 00:26:14.066
OpenTSDB chose a particular
schema to support on Bigtable.

00:26:14.066 --> 00:26:16.440
And here you can see the row
key itself is quite complex.

00:26:16.440 --> 00:26:19.630
It includes the metric, a
timestamp, and key value pairs

00:26:19.630 --> 00:26:20.740
for the tags.

00:26:20.740 --> 00:26:23.490
Note that in front of the
row key, they added a salt.

00:26:23.490 --> 00:26:25.750
This is to help
distribute the load, such

00:26:25.750 --> 00:26:28.000
that if you have
metrics that maybe have

00:26:28.000 --> 00:26:31.000
similar keys, that
you don't hotspot,

00:26:31.000 --> 00:26:33.100
since we're keeping
everything in sorted order.

00:26:33.100 --> 00:26:36.100
And then the data is stored
in the different cells

00:26:36.100 --> 00:26:39.470
of the columns of that row.

00:26:39.470 --> 00:26:41.980
So now you can see that with
the integrations that Bigtable

00:26:41.980 --> 00:26:44.610
provides for both time
series with OpenTSDB

00:26:44.610 --> 00:26:47.140
and graphs with JanusGraph,
we can support the use

00:26:47.140 --> 00:26:49.110
cases that Brian
spoke about earlier

00:26:49.110 --> 00:26:51.780
in the different industries.

00:26:51.780 --> 00:26:54.750
So Bigtable provides both
of these types of use cases

00:26:54.750 --> 00:26:57.060
a fully managed
petabyte-scale database

00:26:57.060 --> 00:26:59.880
that scales linearly
for increased throughput

00:26:59.880 --> 00:27:01.830
while also providing
low latency because

00:27:01.830 --> 00:27:05.640
of the sorted nature of keys and
the ease of finding the keys.

00:27:05.640 --> 00:27:07.960
And it's great for the time
series and graph use cases

00:27:07.960 --> 00:27:09.174
that we have discussed.

00:27:09.174 --> 00:27:10.590
So let's talk
about, what would it

00:27:10.590 --> 00:27:14.010
look like to deploy a combined
architecture of both time

00:27:14.010 --> 00:27:17.860
series and graph on
GCP with Bigtable?

00:27:17.860 --> 00:27:20.700
So here you have a
sample application

00:27:20.700 --> 00:27:24.450
that is using both JanusGraph
as well as OpenTSDB.

00:27:24.450 --> 00:27:26.640
OpenTSDB, JanusGraph,
and Elastic

00:27:26.640 --> 00:27:29.220
are all running
containerized on Kubernetes.

00:27:29.220 --> 00:27:30.642
They're both using Bigtable.

00:27:30.642 --> 00:27:32.850
Obviously, each one of them
will have a separate data

00:27:32.850 --> 00:27:34.240
set encoded in different ways.

00:27:34.240 --> 00:27:35.700
So they can use
different tables,

00:27:35.700 --> 00:27:38.410
but they can use the same
Bigtable table instance.

00:27:38.410 --> 00:27:42.390
We have an application that
is providing an API that

00:27:42.390 --> 00:27:43.620
is talking to JanusGraph.

00:27:43.620 --> 00:27:45.780
And we have data coming
in through Pub/Sub

00:27:45.780 --> 00:27:48.630
that is being consumed by data
flow that is both publishing

00:27:48.630 --> 00:27:52.290
the time series data to OpenTSDB
as well as sending requests

00:27:52.290 --> 00:27:54.940
to the API server.

00:27:54.940 --> 00:27:58.380
Here's an example of how
you would deploy OpenTSDB.

00:27:58.380 --> 00:28:01.590
We have a similar solution,
as we talked about earlier,

00:28:01.590 --> 00:28:02.437
for JanusGraph.

00:28:02.437 --> 00:28:04.020
There's also a simple
way of deploying

00:28:04.020 --> 00:28:07.170
OpenTSDB be together with
Bigtable using Kubernetes

00:28:07.170 --> 00:28:09.000
on Google Cloud.

00:28:09.000 --> 00:28:12.060
And we've open sourced a
Helm chart for JanusGraph.

00:28:12.060 --> 00:28:13.680
That makes it really
easy to deploy

00:28:13.680 --> 00:28:15.137
JanusGraph on Kubernetes.

00:28:15.137 --> 00:28:16.470
This is open source from GitHub.

00:28:16.470 --> 00:28:21.150
And we will have pointers at
the end where you can find it.

00:28:21.150 --> 00:28:24.090
And our partners at Expero wrote
this Container Graph Sample

00:28:24.090 --> 00:28:24.900
Service.

00:28:24.900 --> 00:28:27.025
That is the application
that I spoke about earlier.

00:28:27.025 --> 00:28:29.220
It's a drop wizard-based
demo application.

00:28:29.220 --> 00:28:31.710
It is also open
source on GitHub.

00:28:31.710 --> 00:28:33.720
And what it does
is it's ingesting

00:28:33.720 --> 00:28:37.260
metrics data, the data that
is coming in, to Pub/Sub.

00:28:37.260 --> 00:28:39.942
And the application that I
spoke about earlier, that

00:28:39.942 --> 00:28:41.400
is the application
that they wrote.

00:28:41.400 --> 00:28:42.858
That is the container
graph service

00:28:42.858 --> 00:28:44.790
that is contacted by
data flow and that

00:28:44.790 --> 00:28:46.620
is talking to JanusGraph.

00:28:46.620 --> 00:28:48.360
And what this
application implements

00:28:48.360 --> 00:28:52.410
itself is a graph API, where you
can add and remove containers.

00:28:52.410 --> 00:28:54.657
You can find upstream
and downstream services.

00:28:54.657 --> 00:28:55.990
You can gather the dependencies.

00:28:55.990 --> 00:28:58.448
So you can do that graph walk
that Brian mentioned earlier.

00:28:58.448 --> 00:29:00.300
If something happens
to a particular node,

00:29:00.300 --> 00:29:02.633
how can I find the upstream
and downstream dependencies?

00:29:02.633 --> 00:29:03.660
What will be affected?

00:29:03.660 --> 00:29:05.243
And you can add and
remove containers.

00:29:05.243 --> 00:29:08.900
So this architecture is both
a deployment architecture

00:29:08.900 --> 00:29:10.900
of what you might deploy
on Google Cloud as well

00:29:10.900 --> 00:29:13.930
as the graph that it will
encode upon deployment.

00:29:13.930 --> 00:29:18.640
So it models itself
as a graph, very meta.

00:29:18.640 --> 00:29:20.520
So how would you
connect to JanusGraph?

00:29:20.520 --> 00:29:23.550
As we mentioned, JanusGraph
works with TinkerPop.

00:29:23.550 --> 00:29:25.620
And TinkerPop project
provides a number

00:29:25.620 --> 00:29:27.570
of drivers in the
different languages.

00:29:27.570 --> 00:29:32.040
And there are project-supported
client libraries in .NET,

00:29:32.040 --> 00:29:36.090
JavaScript, Java, and Python,
as well as third party-supported

00:29:36.090 --> 00:29:39.210
Scala, Clojure,
and Go libraries.

00:29:39.210 --> 00:29:42.690
When you write queries in
TinkerPop in the TinkerPop

00:29:42.690 --> 00:29:45.090
library, the queries
themselves are not text.

00:29:45.090 --> 00:29:47.501
They're very fluent, and
they're written to the language

00:29:47.501 --> 00:29:48.250
that you're using.

00:29:48.250 --> 00:29:49.752
So in this case,
in Java, this is

00:29:49.752 --> 00:29:50.960
what a query would look like.

00:29:50.960 --> 00:29:53.730
So you get all the
benefits of your IDE,

00:29:53.730 --> 00:29:55.800
all of the type
checking system, rather

00:29:55.800 --> 00:29:58.110
than combining together
concatenating strings

00:29:58.110 --> 00:30:00.952
and then sending them to
be processed by the server.

00:30:00.952 --> 00:30:02.910
In this case, the query
that we're showing here

00:30:02.910 --> 00:30:04.260
is not very graphy.

00:30:04.260 --> 00:30:05.350
It's not very complicated.

00:30:05.350 --> 00:30:07.270
This is just for
illustration purposes.

00:30:07.270 --> 00:30:08.880
Many of the queries that
you'll see in practice

00:30:08.880 --> 00:30:10.421
will be more
complicated and there'll

00:30:10.421 --> 00:30:11.620
be quite a bit more to them.

00:30:11.620 --> 00:30:13.870
In this case, the query
that you'll see here,

00:30:13.870 --> 00:30:16.460
this just retrieves a list
of running containers.

00:30:16.460 --> 00:30:19.380
But there's much more
you can do with this.

00:30:19.380 --> 00:30:20.689
A query in graphs.

00:30:20.689 --> 00:30:21.480
So this is Gremlin.

00:30:21.480 --> 00:30:22.980
This is the graph
query language.

00:30:22.980 --> 00:30:24.540
It's actually
called a traversal.

00:30:24.540 --> 00:30:26.790
Internally, it runs
a state machine.

00:30:26.790 --> 00:30:28.962
And there are a bunch
of steps to a traversal.

00:30:28.962 --> 00:30:31.170
And they will fall into one
of these five categories.

00:30:31.170 --> 00:30:32.850
So when you see at
the bottom here,

00:30:32.850 --> 00:30:34.750
you have G, which is a graph.

00:30:34.750 --> 00:30:36.100
You will select vertices.

00:30:36.100 --> 00:30:37.240
This is a map phase.

00:30:37.240 --> 00:30:39.840
You will filter define
a specific vertex.

00:30:39.840 --> 00:30:43.080
You will find out all the
outgoing edges by label.

00:30:43.080 --> 00:30:45.990
And then you'll select
a particular attribute

00:30:45.990 --> 00:30:46.740
on the outgoing.

00:30:46.740 --> 00:30:51.180
And just so this will find you
all the names of all the people

00:30:51.180 --> 00:30:55.500
that are known by that
person in that first segment.

00:30:55.500 --> 00:31:00.660
So as you've seen, you can
use both graph and time series

00:31:00.660 --> 00:31:03.410
databases to solve a number
of different problems

00:31:03.410 --> 00:31:05.410
in a number of different
industry verticals.

00:31:05.410 --> 00:31:07.710
This will give you both
the micro view as well as

00:31:07.710 --> 00:31:09.960
the macro view of
the situation, allow

00:31:09.960 --> 00:31:12.679
you to explore the data at a
high level through a graph,

00:31:12.679 --> 00:31:14.220
and then dive deep
to understand what

00:31:14.220 --> 00:31:17.160
is the low-level behavior of the
system at the micro level using

00:31:17.160 --> 00:31:18.420
the time series.

00:31:18.420 --> 00:31:20.760
And JanusGraph and
OpenTSDB provide

00:31:20.760 --> 00:31:24.881
scalable implementations of both
the graph and time series data

00:31:24.881 --> 00:31:25.380
models.

00:31:25.380 --> 00:31:27.840
And they can be both scaled
as stateless services

00:31:27.840 --> 00:31:30.390
on top of a scalable
database, such as Bigtable.

00:31:33.210 --> 00:31:35.400
As next steps, as
Brian and I mentioned,

00:31:35.400 --> 00:31:37.110
there are a number
of resources that you

00:31:37.110 --> 00:31:40.569
can take a look at to learn
more about both everything

00:31:40.569 --> 00:31:42.360
that we've talked about
as well as find all

00:31:42.360 --> 00:31:44.066
the demos that we've mentioned.

00:31:44.066 --> 00:31:46.440
So first, there's the Cloud
Bigtable home page, where you

00:31:46.440 --> 00:31:48.240
can learn more about Bigtable.

00:31:48.240 --> 00:31:49.742
Regarding JanusGraph,
the first link

00:31:49.742 --> 00:31:52.200
there is a solution of how to
run JanusGraph with Bigtable.

00:31:52.200 --> 00:31:54.590
That is the architecture
that I showed earlier.

00:31:54.590 --> 00:31:57.360
There's the Expero
demo container graph

00:31:57.360 --> 00:31:59.130
project that you'll find next.

00:31:59.130 --> 00:32:02.760
And JanusGraph.org has a number
of pointers to both our users

00:32:02.760 --> 00:32:03.490
and use cases.

00:32:03.490 --> 00:32:04.500
Who's running it?

00:32:04.500 --> 00:32:05.576
How they're running it.

00:32:05.576 --> 00:32:06.450
What does it support?

00:32:06.450 --> 00:32:09.540
What language versions that
you might want to use with it.

00:32:09.540 --> 00:32:12.155
Regarding Gremlin, the
graph query language,

00:32:12.155 --> 00:32:13.530
there's an excellent
introduction

00:32:13.530 --> 00:32:16.290
to the Gremlin language
by Kelvin Lawrence.

00:32:16.290 --> 00:32:17.580
That is the first link there.

00:32:17.580 --> 00:32:18.840
It's an entire book.

00:32:18.840 --> 00:32:19.710
It's open source.

00:32:19.710 --> 00:32:20.590
It's also on GitHub.

00:32:20.590 --> 00:32:22.470
But you can read it at this URL.

00:32:22.470 --> 00:32:24.932
It provides an in-depth
tutorial that you probably

00:32:24.932 --> 00:32:25.890
want to take a look at.

00:32:25.890 --> 00:32:27.600
If you're going to
use a property graph,

00:32:27.600 --> 00:32:29.100
you want to learn Gremlin.

00:32:29.100 --> 00:32:30.870
And there's also
a learning program

00:32:30.870 --> 00:32:32.820
from our partners at
Expero that will also

00:32:32.820 --> 00:32:35.400
help you understand or learn
Gremlin and become a Gremlin

00:32:35.400 --> 00:32:36.220
expert.

00:32:36.220 --> 00:32:38.310
There are obviously also
forums and mailing lists,

00:32:38.310 --> 00:32:40.018
where, if you have
complicated questions,

00:32:40.018 --> 00:32:41.550
you can ask questions
about Gremlin.

00:32:41.550 --> 00:32:43.750
And there's Stack Overflow--

00:32:43.750 --> 00:32:47.170
very active users
on Stack Overflow

00:32:47.170 --> 00:32:49.620
who are also developers
of the Gremlin language.

00:32:49.620 --> 00:32:51.340
Regarding OpenTSDB,
as I mentioned,

00:32:51.340 --> 00:32:54.600
we have a solution here
of how to deploy OpenTSDB

00:32:54.600 --> 00:32:56.170
on Google Cloud with Bigtable.

00:32:56.170 --> 00:32:59.430
And there's also a number of
visualizations in the gallery

00:32:59.430 --> 00:33:01.200
that Expero put
together that you

00:33:01.200 --> 00:33:02.550
might want to take a look at.

